		<skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">Small glial cell</skos:altLabel>
		<skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">Set of microglial cells</skos:altLabel>
		<skos:altLabel xml:lang="fr">Microglia</skos:altLabel>
		<skos:altLabel xml:lang="fr">Cellule microgliale</skos:altLabel>
		<skos:altLabel xml:lang="fr">Cellules microgliales</skos:altLabel>
		<skos:altLabel xml:lang="fr">microgliocyte</skos:altLabel>
		<skos:altLabel xml:lang="fr">microglial</skos:altLabel>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">The third type of glial cell, along with astrocytes and oligodendrocytes (which together form the macroglia). Microglia vary in appearance depending on developmental stage, functional state, and anatomical location; subtype terms include ramified, perivascular, ameboid, resting, and activated. Microglia clearly are capable of phagocytosis and play an important role in a wide spectrum of neuropathologies. They have also been suggested to act in several other roles including in secretion (e.g., of (source : MSH)</skos:definition>
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="&OntoAD;C2327597"/>
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="&OntoAD;C1181163"/>
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="&OntoAD;C1267793"/>
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="&OntoAD;C0031307"/>
